{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"680267d16e58f044","name":"card_event_rm_bersaglieri","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Italy/events/card_event_rm_bersaglieri","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"RM BERSAGLIERE","text":"Draw a card from your deck that did not start there. Give your HQ defense equal to its cost.","flavorText":"The Italian destroyer Bersagliere was a Soldati-class vessel commissioned in 1939. Named after the elite Italian light infantry, she embodied speed and firepower. With a top speed of 34 knots and a crew of 206, Bersagliere was equipped with twin 120 mm guns, a dedicated star shell gun for night combat and anti-aircraft defenses. She operated primarily in the Mediterranean, serving in escort and fleet operations during Italy's naval campaigns. Part of the first batch of Soldati-class destroyers, Bersagliere featured several improvements over previous designs, including enhanced stability and fire control. These destroyers were vital in countering Allied convoys and supporting Axis operations across North Africa and beyond. On 7 January 1943, while anchored in the port of Palermo, Bersagliere was struck by Allied aircraft. A direct hit caused a catastrophic explosion, likely from her own torpedoes or depth charges, which destroyed the vessel. The blast split the ship in two, killing 59 crewmen. Her sinking reflected the growing dominance of Allied air power in the Mediterranean by 1943.","faction":"Italy","type":"order","rarity":"Common","cost":1,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"GameplayTags":["subtype.navy"],"Name":"card_event_rm_bersaglieri","title":{"Namespace":"card_italy","Key":"card_event_rm_bersaglieri_title","SourceString":"RM BERSAGLIERE","LocalizedString":"RM BERSAGLIERE"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Italy","kredits":1,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_italy","Key":"card_event_rm_bersaglieri_text","SourceString":"Draw a card from your deck that did not start there. Give your HQ defense equal to its cost.","LocalizedString":"Draw a card from your deck that did not start there. Give your HQ defense equal to its cost."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_italy","Key":"card_event_rm_bersaglieri_history","SourceString":"The Italian destroyer Bersagliere was a Soldati-class vessel commissioned in 1939. Named after the elite Italian light infantry, she embodied speed and firepower. With a top speed of 34 knots and a crew of 206, Bersagliere was equipped with twin 120 mm guns, a dedicated star shell gun for night combat and anti-aircraft defenses. She operated primarily in the Mediterranean, serving in escort and fleet operations during Italy's naval campaigns. Part of the first batch of Soldati-class destroyers, Bersagliere featured several improvements over previous designs, including enhanced stability and fire control. These destroyers were vital in countering Allied convoys and supporting Axis operations across North Africa and beyond. On 7 January 1943, while anchored in the port of Palermo, Bersagliere was struck by Allied aircraft. A direct hit caused a catastrophic explosion, likely from her own torpedoes or depth charges, which destroyed the vessel. The blast split the ship in two, killing 59 crewmen. Her sinking reflected the growing dominance of Allied air power in the Mediterranean by 1943.","LocalizedString":"The Italian destroyer Bersagliere was a Soldati-class vessel commissioned in 1939. Named after the elite Italian light infantry, she embodied speed and firepower. With a top speed of 34 knots and a crew of 206, Bersagliere was equipped with twin 120 mm guns, a dedicated star shell gun for night combat and anti-aircraft defenses. She operated primarily in the Mediterranean, serving in escort and fleet operations during Italy's naval campaigns. Part of the first batch of Soldati-class destroyers, Bersagliere featured several improvements over previous designs, including enhanced stability and fire control. These destroyers were vital in countering Allied convoys and supporting Axis operations across North Africa and beyond. On 7 January 1943, while anchored in the port of Palermo, Bersagliere was struck by Allied aircraft. A direct hit caused a catastrophic explosion, likely from her own torpedoes or depth charges, which destroyed the vessel. The blast split the ship in two, killing 59 crewmen. Her sinking reflected the growing dominance of Allied air power in the Mediterranean by 1943."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Common","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::Homefront","soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'War_UI_Drum_Movement_Organic_War_Drums_2'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/War_UI/Waves/Drum_Movements/War_UI_Drum_Movement_Organic_War_Drums_2.0"},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/RM_BERSAGLIERE.RM_BERSAGLIERE","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":312.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_italy","Key":"card_event_rm_bersaglieri_photo","SourceString":"The Italian Soldati-class destroyer Bersagliere in 1941.","LocalizedString":"The Italian Soldati-class destroyer Bersagliere in 1941."},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackOrder.BP_AttackOrder_C","SubPathString":""}},"variables":[{"name":"possibleUnits","type":"TArray<UBaseCardObject*>","direction":"in"},{"name":"tmpCard","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"in"}],"functions":[{"name":"ExecuteUbergraph_card_event_rm_bersaglieri","owner":"card_event_rm_bersaglieri_C","params":[{"name":"EntryPoint","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"pure":false,"event":false,"flags":"FUNC_Final | FUNC_UbergraphFunction | FUNC_HasDefaults","locals":[{"name":"Temp_text_Variable","type":"FText","direction":"in"},{"name":"CallFunc_getAndDecryptKredit_decryptedKredit","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"Temp_int_Array_Index_Variable","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"Temp_int_Loop_Counter_Variable","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name"